figdown 0.1 table
# `ROW-HIGHLIGHT-CELL-FILL-COLLISION`: the row tint (`highlight`) and a cell `fill=` paint the
# SAME channel, and both ways of writing them for one cell used to resolve
# silently and in opposite directions. Two keys for one channel with an
# undocumented precedence is the `color=` and the §8.4 fill=/stroke= defect;
# the answer here is the same as there — a named line error, never a
# precedence rule (a precedence rule is exactly what `STYLE-KEY-SCOPE` had just removed
# from `field`).
class tinted "vendor extension" fill=#fecaca
table t "T"
| A | B | C |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 2 | 3 |
| 4 | 5 | 6 |
| 7 | 8 | 9 |
# (1) one line, both marks: `highlight` was SILENTLY DISCARDED and never
#     reached the model, while the cell fill drew.
cell (1,1) fill=#eeeeee highlight
# (2) two lines, same cell: both parsed, and the per-cell fill won at paint
#     time — the model said "row 2 is highlighted" while the drawing tinted
#     only part of the row.
cell 2 highlight
cell (2,2) fill=#ffffff
# a class that RESOLVES to a fill collides the same way
cell (2,3) class=tinted
# stroke= and class= without a fill paint a DIFFERENT channel and stay legal
cell (2,1) stroke=#dc2626
# row 3 carries no highlight, so its cell fill is untouched
cell (3,1) fill=#d1fae5
